hadiths[114]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1809","The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: Recite this. « » . The man said: This is for my Lord (as praise for Him), what will be for me? The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: Recite this: ""O Allah, forgive me, have mercy on me, grant me sustenance, and grant me well-being."
"1810","A man came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: I cannot memorise anything from the Qur'an: so teach me something which is sufficient for me. He said: Say Glory be to Allah, and praise be to Allah, and there is no god but Allah, and Allah is most great, and there is no might and no strength but in Allah. :He said: Messenger of Allah, this is for Allah, but what is for me? He said: Say: O Allah have mercy on me, and sustain me, and keep me well, and guide me. When he stood up, he made a sign with his hand (indicating that he had earned a lot). The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: He filed up his hand with virtues. (Using translation from Abū Dāʾūd 832)"
"1811","The four most beloved words to Allah Almighty are: « » ."
"1812","the Prophet ﷺ said: ""(There are) four that are the best of words, and it does not matter with which you begin: Subhan-Allah, wal-Hamdu-Lillah, wa la ilaha illallah, wa Allahu Akbar (Glory is to Allah, praise is to Allah, none has the right to be worshiped but Allah and Allah is the Most Great)."" (Using translation from Ibn Mājah 3811)"
"1813","""I heard Abu Wa'il say: ""A man said in the presence of Abdullah: 'I recited Al-Mufassal in one rak'ah.' He said: 'That is like reciting poetry. I know the similar surahs that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite together.' And he mentioned twenty surahs from Al-Mufassal, two by two in each rak'ah."" (Using translation from Nasāʾī 1005)"
"1814","I asked my teacher about this hadith a second time, so he narrated these words: He heard the Prophet (peace be upon him) reciting Surah Qaf."
"1815","The narrator states that Ibn Sa’ib was present there at that time."
"1816","The narrator states that later the Prophet (peace be upon him) would perform a relatively shorter prayer."
"1817","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar, may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) would sometimes lead us in the Fajr prayer and recite Surah As-Saffat."
"1818","narrates that the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) led them in the morning prayer and recited Al-Mu'awwidhatayn (Surah Al-Falaq and Surah An-Nas)."
"1819","I said the dawn prayer behind the Apostle of ﷺ and heard him reciting: 'Nay. I call to witness the stars, running their courses and setting"" (al-Qur'an, lxxxi. 15-16) and Done of us bent his back till he completed prostration. (Using translation from Muslim 475)"
"1820","“For the Subh prayer on Fridays, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite ‘Alif-Lam-Mim. The revelation...’ [32:1] and ‘Has there not been over man...’” [76:1] (Using translation from Ibn Mājah 821)"
"1821","It was narrated from Abu Hurairah that for the Subh prayer on Fridays, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite ‘Alif-Lam-Mim’. The revelation...’[32:1] and ‘Has there not been over man...” [76:1] (Using translation from Ibn Mājah 823)"
"1822","The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite between sixty and one hundred verses in Al-Ghadah (Subh) prayer. (Using translation from Nasāʾī 948)"
"1823","The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, used to perform a brief prayer. He, peace and blessings be upon him, would recite Surah Waqi’ah and similar surahs in the Fajr prayer."
"1824","narrates about the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): During the Zuhr prayer, the people would hear the soft recitation of Surah Al-A'la and Surah Al-Ghashiyah from the tongue of the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"1825","narrates that the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) used to recite approximately thirty verses in each of the first two units (rak'ahs) during the standing (qiyam) of the Zuhr prayer. And in one of the last two units, he would recite fifteen verses. In the first two units of the Asr prayer, he would recite about fifteen verses in each unit, and in each of the last two units, he would recite about half of that amount."
"1826","We asked Khabbab: Did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recit (the Quran) in the noon and afternoon prayers? He replied: Yes. We then asked: How did you know this? He said: By the shaking of his beard, may peace be upon him. (Using translation from Abū Dāʾūd 801)"
"1827","The Prophet ﷺ used to recite ""By the heaven holding the big stars"" and: ""By the heaven, and At-Tariq (the night-comer, i.e. the bright star)"" and similar surahs in Zuhr and 'Asr. (Using translation from Nasāʾī 979)"
"1828","We used to estimate how long the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood in the noon and the afternoon prayer, and we estimated that he stood in the first two rak’ahs of the noon prayer as long as it takes to recite thirty verses (of the Qur’an), such as A-L-M Tanzil al-Sajdah. And we estimated that he stood in the last two rak’ahs half the time he stood in the first two rak’ahs. We estimated that he stood in the first two rak’ahs of the afternoon prayer as long as he did in the last two at noon; and we estimated that he stood in the last two rak’ahs of the afternoon prayer half the time he did in first two. (Using translation from Abū Dāʾūd 804)"
"1829","The Messenger of Allah ﷺ would recite in the first two rak'ahs of the noon and afternoon prayers the opening chapter of the Book and another surah. He would sometimes recite loud enough to make audible to us the verse and would recite in the last two rak'ahs Surat al-Faitiha (only). (Using translation from Muslim 451b)"
"1830","“I said to Khabbab: ‘How did you recognize that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was reciting in the Zuhr and the ‘Asr?’ He said: ‘From the movement of his beard.’” (Using translation from Ibn Mājah 826)"
"1831","""My father told us that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to recite Umm Al-Quran and two surahs in the first two rak'ahs of Zuhr and 'Asr, and he would make us hear a verse sometimes, and he used to make the first rak'ah lengthy."" (Using translation from Nasāʾī 975)"
"1832","I heard Ibn’Abbas reciting wa’l-mursalat urfan(surah lxxxvii). She said; sonny you have reminded me of this surah by your recitation. Thie is the last surah which I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ reciting in the sunset prayer. (Using translation from Abū Dāʾūd 810)"
"1833","She heard the Prophet ﷺ recite Al-Mursalat in Maghrib. (Using translation from Nasāʾī 986)"
"1834","I came regarding the ransom of the people of Badr. I heard the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) at that time; he was leading the people in the Maghrib prayer, and he was reciting Surah At-Tur."
"1835","The Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) was leading those people in the Maghrib prayer, and he recited this verse: ""Those who disbelieved and hindered (others) from the way of Allah"" (that is, he recited Surah Muhammad)."
"1836","I swear by the name of Allah that the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) used to recite the longer of the two long surahs in this prayer, that is, he used to recite Alif Lam Meem Saad (i.e., Surah Al-A'raf)."
"1837","""I have never prayed behind anyone whose prayer more closely resembled that of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ than so-and-so."" (The narrator) Sulaiman siad: ""He used to make the first two rak'ahs of Zuhr lengthy and the last two shorter, and he would make 'Asr shorter; in Maghrib he would recite the short Mufassal surahs, in Isha' the medium-length Mufassal surahs and in Subh the long Mufassal surahs. (Using translation from Nasāʾī 982)"
"1838","narrates regarding the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him): He was traveling, and in one of the two rak'ahs of the 'Isha prayer, he recited Surah At-Tin."
"1839","The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) instructed Sayyiduna Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him) to recite Surah Ash-Shams, Surah Al-Lail, Surah Al-A'la, and Surah Ad-Duha or similar surahs in the 'Isha prayer."
"1840","I will go to the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) and inform him about this. Then he went to the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) and said: Sayyiduna Mu'adh performs prayer behind you, then comes back and leads us in prayer. Last night, you performed the prayer late. Sayyiduna Mu'adh came and started leading us in prayer, and he began reciting Surah Al-Baqarah. I stepped back and performed the prayer alone. O Messenger of Allah! We are people who draw water with camels and work with our own hands. The Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) said: O Mu'adh, do you want to put people to trial? When you lead these people in prayer, recite Surah Al-Lail, Surah Al-A'la, and Surah Al-Buruj."
"1841","The Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) used to recite Surah Al-Kafiroon and Surah Al-Ikhlas in the Maghrib prayer on the night of Jumu'ah, and in the Isha prayer he used to recite Surah Al-Jumu'ah and Surah Al-Munafiqun."
"1842","The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Uqbah bin Amir! You will not recite any surah that is more beloved to Allah Almighty and reaches Him more than Surah Al-Falaq. If you are able, then recite it in every prayer. (Imam Ibn Hibban, may Allah have mercy on him, says:) The kunyah of Aslam bin Imran is Abu Imran; he was among the people of Egypt and among the Tabi‘een there."
"1843","I was also wondering what was the reason that (in the recitation of) the Qur'an, there was an obstruction for me."
"1844","The Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) led his companions in prayer. When he completed the prayer, he turned towards the peopl"
"1845","The Prophet (peace and blessin"
"1846","I did. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: I had realized that one of you was causing an interruption for me in the recitation."
"1847","The Messenger of Allah ﷺ observed the Zuhr prayer and a person recited Sabbih Isma Rabbik al-a'la (Glorify the name of thy Lord, the Most High) behind him. When he (the Holy Pro- phet) concluded the prayer he said: Who amongst you recited (the above-mentioned verse) or who amongst you was the reciter? A person said: It was I. Upon this he (the Holy Prophet) observed: I thought as if someone amongst you was disputing with me (in what I was reciting). (Using translation from Muslim 398b)"
"1848","""Messenger of Allah prayed the Subh prayer, and he had difficulty with the recitation. When turned (after finishing) he said: 'I think that you are reciting behind your Imam?'"" He said: ""We said: 'Yes, Messenger of Allah, by Allah!' He said: 'Do not do that, except for Umm Al-Kitab, for there is no Salat for one who does not recite it.'"" (Using translation from Tirmidhī 311)"
"1849","""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished a prayer in which he recited out loud, then he said: 'Did any one of you recite with me just now?' A man said: 'Yes, O Messenger of Allah.' He said: 'I was wondering what was distracting me in reciting Quran.'"" So the people stopped reciting in prayers in which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited out loud when they heard that. (Using translation from Nasāʾī 919)"
"1850","I was also wondering what was the reason, (that in the recitation of the Qur'an) there was competition with me. The narrator says: They took admonition from this, and (after that, in his congregation) people would not recite (behind him)."
"1851","I was also wondering what was the reason that there was competition with me in the recitation of the Qur'an."" Zuhri narrates: The Muslims refrained from this, and they would not recite while following him (the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him). Imam Abu Hatim states: This narration is famous as being transmitted from Zuhri, which his companions have narrated from Ibn Akimah from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him). In this, Imam Awza'i made a mistake, because even a horse may stumble. He said: This is narrated from Zuhri from Sa'id bin al-Musayyib. Walid bin Muslim knew that he (Awza'i) had made a mistake, so he said: This is narrated from the person who heard it from Sayyiduna Abu Hurairah (may Allah be pleased with him). He did not mention Sa'id. As for Zuhri's statement that people refrained from reciting, it means that they did not recite aloud behind the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), following his prohibition. When the Imam is reciting aloud, this is mentioned in the statement of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him): ""What is the reason that there is dispute with me in the recitation of the Qur'an?"
"1852","The people remained silent. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) repeated this three times, then one person said (or perhaps the people said): ""Yes, we do so."" The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Do not do so. Each of you should recite Surah Al-Fatihah in his heart."" (Imam Ibn Hibban, may Allah have mercy on him, says:) Abu Qilabah heard this narration from Muhammad bin Abu Aishah, who narrated it from a companion of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), and he also heard this narration from Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), so both chains are preserved."
"1853","The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would recite aloud in those prayers in which he made us hear, we make you hear; and in those prayers in which he kept it hidden from us, we keep it hidden from you."
"1854","The iqamah for the prayer would be pronounced for the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), then one of us would go to Baqi’ (the field) to relieve himself. Then he would return, perform ablution, and catch the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) in the first rak’ah of the Zuhr prayer."
"1855","The Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) used to perform the first two rak‘ahs of Fajr and Dhuhr prayers at length. The narrator states: We think that he did this so that people could join (the congregational prayer)."
"1856","It was narrated from Anas that the Prophet ﷺ used to make his prayer very brief but still complete when leading people. (Using translation from Nasāʾī 824)"
"1857","The Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) would recite (Qur'an) in the first two units (rak'ahs) while leading us in the Zuhr prayer. He would make the first rak'ah long and the second one (relatively) shorter."
"1858","We used to estimate how long the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood in the noon and the afternoon prayer, and we estimated that he stood in the first two rak’ahs of the noon prayer as long as it takes to recite thirty verses (of the Qur’an), such as A-L-M Tanzil al-Sajdah. And we estimated that he stood in the last two rak’ahs half the time he stood in the first two rak’ahs. We estimated that he stood in the first two rak’ahs of the afternoon prayer as long as he did in the last two at noon; and we estimated that he stood in the last two rak’ahs of the afternoon prayer half the time he did in first two. (Using translation from Abū Dāʾūd 804)"
"1859","The narrator says: Sayyiduna Sa’d (may Allah be pleased with him) became angry and said: O Allah! If this man is lying, then lengthen his life, increase his poverty, and subject him to trials. The narrator says: The narrator named Umair has reported this. He saw that due to old age, the man’s eyebrows were drooping over his eyes, he was extremely poor, and he was afflicted with trials, but he did not get anything. He was asked: O Abu Sa’dah! What is your condition? He used to say: An old man who is afflicted with trials. The supplication of Sayyiduna Sa’d against me has been accepted."
"1860","Sayyiduna Wa'il bin Hujr Hadrami (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: I thought to myself that I would definitely observe how the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) performed the prayer. I observed him: when he stood up, he said the takbeer and raised his hands until he brought them level with his ears. He placed his right blessed hand on the back of his left hand’s palm, wrist, and forearm. Then, when he intended to go into ruku‘, he raised both his hands to the same extent (as he d"
"1861","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) narrates: When the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) began the prayer, he would raise both hands up to the shoulders. When he said the takbeer to go into bowing (ruku‘), and when he raised his head from bowing, he would raise them in the same way, and he would say: “Allah has heard the one who praised Him. Our Lord, to You belongs all praise.” The Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) would not do this (i.e., raise"
"1862","The narrator named Ibn Jahadah states: I mentioned this to Hasan bin Abu’l-Hasan, so he said: This is the prayer of the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him); whoever wanted to do so did it, and whoever wanted to leave it left it. Imam Abu Hatim (may Allah have mercy on him) states: The narrator named Muhammad bin Jahadah is among the trustworthy and precise narrators. He is a person of virtue regarding religiousness; however, he made a mistake regarding the name of this narrator, because even a horse can stumble. He said: This is Wa’il bin Alqamah, whereas it is Alqamah bin Wa’il."
"1863","narrates that when the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) began the prayer, he would raise both his hands while saying the takbeer, until both hands reached the level of his ears. He would do the same when going into ruku‘ and when rising from ruku‘."
"1864","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar, may Allah be pleased with him): I saw the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) tha"
"1865","Sayyiduna Abu Humayd (may Allah be pleased with him) say in the presence of ten Companions, among whom was Sayyiduna Abu Qatadah (may"
"1866","The people said: Then show us. The narrator says that then he stood up to perform the prayer. The others were watching. He said the takbir at the beginning. He raised both hands up to the shoulders, then he said the takbir to go into ruku’. Then he performed raf’ al-yadayn. Then he placed both hands on his knees. He did not lower his head completely nor raise it up. Then he raised his head and recited « » . Then he performed raf’ al-yadayn. Saying « » , he went into prostration. He placed both his palms, both knees, and the edges of both feet on the ground while in the state of prostration. Then he said the takbir and sat down. He spread one leg as tawarruk and kept the other foot upright. Then, saying the takbir, he performed the second prostration, said the takbir, and stood up, but he did not do tawarruk. Then he did the same and performed the second rak’ah. In this way, he said the takbir and after performing two rak’ahs, he sat down. Then, when he intended to stand up, he said the takbir. Then he performed the last two rak’ahs in the same way. Then, when he finished with salam, he first turned to the right and said: “As-salamu ‘alaykum wa rahmatullah,” and turning to the left, he said: “As-salamu ‘alaykum wa rahmatullah.” Hasan bin Harnami, the narrator, has reported this. ‘Isa, the narrator, narrated this hadith to me. In this hadith, regarding the tashahhud, these words are also present: He placed his left hand on his left thigh and his right hand on his right thigh, and then he pointed with one finger. Imam Abu Hatim (may Allah have mercy on him) states: Muhammad bin ‘Amr heard this narration from Sayyiduna Abu Humayd Sa’idi (may Allah be pleased with him). He also heard this narration from Abbas bin Sahal from his father. Both their chains are preserved."
"1867","‘While he was among ten of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ including Abu Qatadah: “I heard Abu Humaid As-Sa’idi say: ‘I am the most knowledgeable of you concerning the prayer of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.’ They said: ‘Why? By Allah, you did not follow him more than we did, and you did not accompany him for longer.’ He said: ‘Yes I am.’ They said: ‘Show us.’ He said: ‘When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood up for prayer, he would say the Takbir, then he would raise his hands parallel to his shoulders, and every part of his body would settle in place. Then he would recite, then he would raise his hands parallel to his shoulders and bow, placing his palms on his knees and supporting his weight on them. He neither lowered his head, nor raised it up, it was evenly balanced (between either extreme). Then he would say: “Sami’ Allahu liman hamidah (Allah hears those who praise Him); and he would raise his hands parallel with his shoulders, until every bone returned to its place. Then he would prostrate himself on the ground, keeping his arms away from his sides. Then he would raise his head and tuck his left foot under him and sit on it, and he would spread his toes when he prostrated.* Then he would prostrate, then say the Takbir and sit on his left foot, until every bone returned to its place. Then he would stand up and do the same in the next Rak’ah. Then when he stood up after two Rak’ah, he would raise his hands level with his shoulders as he did at the beginning of the prayer. Then he would offer the rest of his prayer in like manner until, when he did the prostration after which the Taslim comes, he would push one of his feet back and sit with his weight on his left side, Mutawarrikan.’ They said: ‘You have spoken the truth; this is how the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to perform the prayer.’” * Meaning that he would plant them in such a way that they were facing the Qiblah.  Mutawarrikan: i.e., sitting with the left foot brought forward so that one's buttocks are in direct contact with the ground. (Using translation from Ibn Mājah 1061)"
"1868","narrates from his father: When he would start the prayer, he would raise his hands. When he would go into bowing (ruku‘), he would raise his hands. When he would say « » (that is, when he would stand up from bowing), he would raise his hands. When he would stand up after two rak‘ahs, he would also raise both hands up to the shoulders."
"1869","I remember the prayer of the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) better than all of you. I saw the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) when he said the takbeer, he would raise both hands up to the shoulders. When he went into ruku’, he would place both hands on his knees. He would keep his back straight. When he raised his head, he would stand up straight, and when he went into prostration, he would neither spread his arms nor keep them raised. He would direct both his feet towards the qiblah. When he sat in the last rak’ah, he would take out his left leg and sit on his buttocks."
"1870","he (Muhammad) said: ""I heard him saying - while he was among ten of the Companions of the Prophet, one of whom was Abu Qatadah bin Ribi - 'I am the most knowledgeable among you of the Salat of the Messenger of Allah.' They said: 'You did not precede us in his companionship, nor were you in his company more than us.' He said: 'Even still."" They said: 'Go ahead.' So he said: 'When Messenger of Allah stood for Salat he would stand with his back straight and raise his hands until they were at the level of his shoulder. Then he would say: (Allahu Akbar) ""Allah is Most Great"" and bow. Then he would straighten (his back) so that he would not lower his head, nor raise it, and he placed his hands on his knees. Then he said: (Sami Allahu liman hamidah) ""Allah listens to those who praise Him."" And he raised his hands and stood up straight until all of his bones completely returned to their places. Then he went down to the ground prostrating, then he said: (Allahu Akbar) ""Allah is Most Great."" Then he held his upper arms away from his midsection, and opened his toes on his feet (facing the Qiblah), then he bend his left foot and sat on it then straightened up until all of his bones completely returned to their placed, then he went down to prostrate. Then he said: (Allahu Akbar) ""Allah is Most Great,"" then he bent his foot and sat and straightened up until all of his bones completely returned to their places. Then he got up. Then in the second Rak'ah he did the same as that, such that when he stood from the two prostrations, he sad the Takbir and raised his hands until they were at the level of his shoulders as he did when he opened the Salat. Then he did like that until it was the Rak'ah in which his Salat was to end, when he moved his left foot over and sat on his side (in the Mutawarrik postion). Then he said the Taslim.'"" (Using translation from Tirmidhī 304)"
"1871","Abu Humaid, Abu Usaid, Sahl. B Sa’d and Muhammad b. Maslamah (once) got together and discussed how the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to offer his prayer. Abu Humaid said: I am more informed than any of you regarding the prayer offered by the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. Then he mentioned a part of it, and said: He then bowed and placed his hands upon his knees as if he caught hold of them; and bent them, keeping (his arms) away from his sides. He them prostrated himself and placed his nose and forehead (on the ground); and kept his arms away from his side, and placed his palms (on the ground opposite his shoulders; he then raised his head that every bone returned to its proper place; (he then prostrated twice) until he finished this prostrations). Then he sat down and spread out his left foot, putting forward the front of his right foot towards the qiblah placing the palm of his right hand on his right knee, and the palm of his left hand on his left knee, and he pointed with his finger. Abu Dawud said: This tradition has been narrated by Ibn al-Mubarak from Fulaih who heard ‘Abbas . Sahl narrating it; but I do not remember it. I think he made the mention of ‘Isa b. ‘Abd Allah who heard ‘Abbas b. Sahl saying: I accompanied Abu Humaid al-Sa’idi. (Using translation from Abū Dāʾūd 734)"
"1872","We came to the Prophet ﷺ and we were (a few) young men of approximately equal age and stayed with him for twenty nights. Then he thought that we were anxious for our families, and he asked us whom we had left behind to look after our families, and we told him. He was kindhearted and merciful, so he said, ""Return to your families and teach them (religious knowledge) and order them (to do good deeds) and offer your prayers in the way you saw me offering my prayers, and when the stated time for the prayer becomes due, then one of you should pronounce its call (i.e. the Adhan), and the eldest of you should lead you in prayer. (Using translation from Bukhārī 6008)"
"1873","Abu Qilaba reported that he saw Malik b. Huwairith raising his hands at the beginning of prayer and raising his hands before kneeling down, and raising his hands after lifting his head from the state of kneeling, and he narrated that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to do like this. (Using translation from Muslim 391a)"
"1874","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him). He asked us: Have those people performed the prayer? We replied: No. So he said: You both stand and perform the prayer. We stood up to pray behind him. He placed one of us on his right side and the other on his left side. Then he performed the prayer without giving the adhan or the iqamah. During the prayer, when he went into ruku', he interlaced his fingers and placed them between his two knees. When he had completed the prayer, he mentioned this matter. He said: I have seen the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) perform the prayer in this manner. He said: ""O people! Soon there will be rulers appointed over you who will neglect the prayer; they will strangle it as a dying person is strangled. Whoever among you finds this situation, let him perform the prayer at its appointed time, and make his prayer with those people a voluntary one."" (Imam Ibn Hibban, may Allah have mercy on him, says:) Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) used to interlace his fingers during ruku', and he held the opinion that he had seen the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) do so. However, from the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) until our present day, all Muslims are agreed that this act was in the early days of Islam, and then the command to place both hands on the knees during ruku' abrogated it. So, how could such a thing remain hidden from a person of the stature of Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), with all his knowledge, virtue, asceticism, piety, his abundant practice of religious rulings, and his praying in the first row behind the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), as he was among the wise and experienced? Despite all this, how could something that is abrogated by the consensus of all Muslims remain hidden from him? Or perhaps he saw it and then forgot it. So it is also possible that, just as interlacing the fingers during ruku' was hidden from him, the act of raising the hands at the time of ruku' and when rising from ruku' by the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) was also hidden from him, or he saw it and then forgot it."
"1875","""Alqamah and I entered upon 'Abdullah bin Mas'ud and he said to us: 'Have these people prayed?' We said: 'No.' He said: 'Get up and pray.' So we went to stand behind him, and he put one of us on his right and the other on his left, and he prayed with no Adhan and no Iqamah. When he bowed he interlaced his fingers and placed his hands between his knees, and he said: 'I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ doing that.'"" (Using translation from Nasāʾī 719)"
"1876","I am one among you who is more informed of the way the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed. They said: Why, By Allah, you did not follow him more than us, nor did you remain in his company longer than us? He said: Yes. They said: Then describe (how the Prophet prayed). He said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood up to pray, he raised his hands so as to bring them opposite his shoulders, and uttered the takbir (Allah is the most great), until every bone rested in its place properly: then re recited (some verses from the Quran); then he uttered the takbir (Allah is most great), raising his hands so as to bring them opposite his shoulders; then he bowed; placing the palms of his hands on his knees and keeping himself straight, neither raising nor lowering his head; then raised his head saying: “Allah listens to him who praise Him”; then raised his hands so as to bring them exactly opposite to his shoulders; then uttered: “Allah is most great”; then lowered himself to the ground (in prostration), keeping his arms away from his sides; then raised his head, bent his left foot and sat on it, and opened the toes when he prostrated: then he uttered: “Allah is most great”; then raised his head, bent his left foot and sat on it so that every bone returned to its place properly; then he did the same in the second (rak’ah). At the end of the two Rak’ahs he stood up and uttered the takbir (Allah is most great), raising his hands so as to bring them opposite to his shoulders; then he bowed, placing the palms of his hands on his knees and keeping himself straight, neither raising or lowering his head: then raised his head saying: “Allah listens to him who praises Him”; then raised his hands so as to bring them exactly opposite his shoulders; then uttered: “Allah is most great”; then lowered himself to the ground (in prostration), keeping his arms away from his sides; then raised his head, bent his left foot and sat on it, and opened the toes when he prostrated himself; then he prostrated; then uttered: “Allah is most great”; then raised his head, bent his left foot and sat on it so that every bone returned to its place properly; then he did the same in the second (rak’ah). At the end of two rak’ahs he stood up and uttered the takbir (Allah is most great), raising his hands so as to bring them opposite to his shoulders in the way he had uttered the Takbir (Allah is most great) at the beginning of the prayer; then he did that in the remainder of his prayer; and after prostration which if followed by the taslim (salutation) he out his left foot and sat on his left hip. They said: You have spoken the truth. This is how he(peace be upon him) used to pray. (Using translation from Abū Dāʾūd 730)"
"1877","Sayyiduna Abdullah bin Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) narrates about the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him): When he would begin the prayer, he would raise his hands; when he intended to go into bowing (ruku‘); when he would raise his head from bowing; and when he would stand up after performing two units (rak‘ahs), at all these occasions he would raise both hands up to the shoulders."""""
"1878","The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us, and during that time some people raised their hands during prayer. So he sa"
"1879","narrates this about the Noble Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): He came to the mosque and saw some people raising their hands, so he said: ""These people have raised their hands like the tails of unruly horses. You should remain in a state of calmness during prayer."
"1880","I see your hands moving like the tails of unruly horses? It is sufficient for each of you to place both his hands on his knees and then turn to the right and to the left to offer salam."
"1881","I see you raising your hands like the tails of unruly horses? Is it not sufficient for any one of you to place his hand on his knee and then offer salam to the person on his right and the person on his left?"
"1882","I performed prayer beside my father. I placed my hands together between my palms and then put them between my knees. My father forbade me from this and said, ""We used to do this before, then we were forbidden from it, and we were commanded to place (our hands) on the knees."
"1883","Sayyiduna Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him), saw me doing this and said: We used to do this before, then we were forbidden from it, and we were commanded to place (our hands) on the knees."
"1884","The bowing (ruku') of the Prophet (peace be upon him), his raising his head after bowing, his prostration (sajdah), and his sitting between the two prostrations were all approximately equal in duration."
"1885","I will not show any negligence in this regard; I will lead you in prayer just as I saw the Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) leading us in prayer."" Thabit reports: I saw Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) doing something which I do not see you doing; when he would stand up after raising his head from bowing (ruku'), someone could say, ""Perhaps he has forgotten to go into prostration (sajdah)."" And when he would sit after raising his head from the first prostration, someone could say, ""He has perhaps forgotten to go into the second prostration."
"1886","I have not performed prayer behind anyone who offered a prayer more concise and more complete than the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him). Sometimes the Prophet (peace be upon him) would hear the sound of a child crying behind him, so he would shorten the prayer, fearing that the child's mother might fall into trial."
"1887","The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: Sit down. Then a man from the tribe of Thaqif came and said: O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)! There are some words about which I want to know. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: The Ansari has preceded you. The Ansari said: This man is a traveler and the traveler has a right, so you should begin with him. So the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) turned to the Thaqafi man and said: If you wish, I can tell you about the thing you wanted to ask, and if you wish, you may ask me and I will tell you. The man said: O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him)! Tell me about the thing I wanted to ask you. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: You came to ask me about bowing, prostration, prayer, and fasting. He said: Yes! By the One Who sent you with the truth, you have not erred about what was in my mind. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: When you go into bowing, place both your palms on your knees, keep your fingers apart, and remain there until every limb comes to rest in its place. When you go into prostration, firmly place your forehead, do not peck (like a bird), and perform prayer at the beginning and end of the day. He said: O Prophet of Allah! If I pray between these two times, the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: Then you are among those who pray. Fast on the 13th, 14th, and 15th of every month. Then the Thaqafi man stood up, and the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) turned to the Ansari. He said: If you wish, I can tell you about the thing you came to ask, and if you wish, you may ask me and I will inform you. He said: No, O Prophet of Allah! Tell me about the thing I wanted to ask you. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: You came to ask me about the pilgrim—when he leaves his house, what are his rulings; when he stands in Arafat, what are his rulings; when he throws pebbles at the Jamarat, what are his rulings; when he shaves his head, what are his rulings; and when he finally circumambulates the House (Ka'bah), what are his rulings. The man said: O Prophet of Allah! By the One Who sent you with the truth, you have not erred about what was in my mind. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: The ruling for this person is that when he leaves his house, for every step his mount takes, he is given a good deed or one of his sins is erased. When the pilgrim stands in Arafat, Allah Almighty descends to the lowest heaven and says: Look at My servants with disheveled hair and dust-covered bodies. Bear witness, I have forgiven their sins—even if they are as many as the drops of rain or the grains of sand. When the pilgrim throws pebbles at the Jamarat, no one knows what reward he will receive until the Day of Resurrection when his reward will be given in full. When he shaves his head, for every hair that falls from his head, he will be given light on the Day of Resurrection. And when he performs the final circumambulation of the House, he emerges from his sins as on the day his mother gave birth to him."
"1888","The Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The worst thief among people is the one who steals from his prayer."" They asked: How does he steal from his prayer? The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""He does not perform its bowing (ruku') and its prostration (sujud) completely."
"1889","Sayyiduna Ammar bin Yasir (may Allah be pleased with him) performed two rak'ahs. He performed them briefly, so Abdur Rahman bin Harith said to"
"1890","The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) came to the mosque. A man entered the mosque. He performed prayer and then came and sat down. Th"
"1891","The Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) saw, from the corner of his eyes, a person who was not keeping his back straight during bowing (ruku) and prostration (sajda). So the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) said: The prayer of that person is not valid who does not keep his back straight."
"1892","The Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) said: “That prayer is not valid in which a person does not keep his back straight durin"
"1893","The Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) said: “The prayer of that person is not valid who does not keep his back straight durin"
"1894","Sayyiduna Hudhaifah (may Allah be pleased with him) saw a man pecking (in prayer) near the gates of ""Kindah,"" so he asked: ""How long have you been praying like this?"" He replied: ""For forty years."" So Sayyiduna Hudhaifah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""If you die in this state, you will die upon something other than the nature upon which Sayyiduna Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) was sent."" A person should pray briefly, but perform the bowing (ruku') and prostration (sujud) completely."
"1895","The Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade me from reciting (Qur'an) during bowing (ruku') or prostration (sujud)."
"1896","""The Prophet ﷺ drew back the curtain when the people were in rows behind Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: 'O people, there is nothing left of the features of Prophethood except a good dream that a Muslim sees or is seen by others for him.' Then he said: 'Verily, I have been forbidden from reciting the Qur'an when bowing or prostrating. As for bowing, glorify the Lord therein, and as for prostration, strive hard in supplication for it is more deserving of a response.'"" (Using translation from Nasāʾī 1045)"
"1897","I performed prayer behind the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). When he went into bowing (ruku‘), he began to recite « » , and when he went into prostration (sajdah), he recited « » ."
"1898","The name of the uncle of the narrator Musa bin Ayyub is Iyas bin Amir, and he is a trustworthy (thiqah) narrator from Egypt."
"1899","All Glorious, All Holy, Lord of the Angels and the Spirit. (Using translation from Muslim 487a)"
"1900","""The Prophet ﷺ drew back the curtain when the people were in rows behind Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: 'O people, there is nothing left of the features of Prophethood except a good dream that a Muslim sees or is seen by others for him.' Then he said: 'Verily, I have been forbidden from reciting the Qur'an when bowing or prostrating. As for bowing, glorify the Lord therein, and as for prostration, strive hard in supplication for it is more deserving of a response.'"" (Using translation from Nasāʾī 1045)"
"1901","ʿO Allah, to You I have bowed, in You I have believed and to You I have submitted. You are my Lord. My hearing my sight, my brain, my bones and my sinews submit to You. Whatever my feet carry is for Allah, the Lord of the Worlds.” (Using translation from Aḥmad 960)"
"1902","Sayyiduna Anas bin Malik (may Allah be pleased with him). He described to us the manner of the Prophet’s (peace and blessings be upon him) prayer, saying: When the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) was performing prayer, and he would raise his head from bowing (ruku’), due to the length of his standing, we would think that perhaps he had forgotten (to go into prostration)."
"1903","I have bowed for You, I have believed in You, I have submitted to You. My hearing, my sight, my marrow, my bones, and my tendons are humbled before You.” When he would raise his head from bowing, he would recite: “Allah has heard the one who praised Him. O our Lord, all praise is due to You, as much as would fill the heavens and the earth and whatever is between them, and as much as You will after that.”"
"1904","“When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised his head from Ruku’, he said: ‘Sami’ Allahu liman hamidah, Allahumma, Rabbana lakal-hamd, mil’ as-samawati wa mil’ al- ard wa mil’ ma shi’ta min shay’in ba’d (Allah hears those who praise Him. O Allah! O our Lord, to You is the praise as much as fills the heavens, as much as fills the earth and as much as You will after that).’” (Using translation from Ibn Mājah 878)"
"1905","When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: “ Allah listens to him who praises Him,” he also said: O Allah, our Lord, to thee be the praise in all heavens. Mu’ammil said( in his version); “ In all the heavens, and in all the earth, and in all that it pleases Thee to create afterwards. O thou Who art worthy of praise and glory, most worthy of what a servant says, and we are all thy servants, no one can withhold what thou givest or give what Thou withholdest. “The narrators then were agreed on the words: “And riches cannot avail a wealthy person with Thee.” (Using translation from Abū Dāʾūd 847)"
"1906","When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised his head after bowing, he said: Allah! our Lord, to Thee be the praise that would fill the heavens and the earth and that which is between them, and that which will please Thee besides (them). Worthy art Thou of all praise and glory. No one can withhold what Thou givest, or give what Thou withholdest. And the greatness O! the great availeth not against Thee. (Using translation from Muslim 478a)"
"1907","When the Imam says: “Allah listens to him who praised Him,” say: “O Allah, our lord, to Thee be the praise, “ for if what anyone says synchronises with what the angels say, he will be forgiven his past sins. (Using translation from Abū Dāʾūd 848)"
"1908","“When the Imam says: ‘Sami’ Allahu liman hamidah (Allah hears those who praise Him),’ say: ‘Rabbana wa lakal-hamd (O our Lord, to You is the praise).’” (Using translation from Ibn Mājah 876)"
"1909","“When the Imam says: ‘Sami’ Allahu liman hamidah (Allah hears those who praise Him),’ say: ‘Rabbana wa lakal-hamd (O our Lord, to You is the praise).’” (Using translation from Ibn Mājah 876)"
"1910","One day we were praying behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ raised his head after bowing, he said: Allah listened to him who praised Him. A man behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: O Allah, Our Lord, and to Thee be praise, much praise, good and blessed. When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ finished his prayer, he asked: Which of you if the one who spoke (the words) just now. The man said: I (uttered) these words, Prophet of Allah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: I saw more than thirty angels racing against one another to be the one to write them first. (Using translation from Abū Dāʾūd 770)"
"1911","When the Imam says: “Allah listens to him who praised Him,” say: “O Allah, our lord, to Thee be the praise, “ for if what anyone says synchronises with what the angels say, he will be forgiven his past sins. (Using translation from Abū Dāʾūd 848)"
"1912","I saw that the Prophet ﷺ placed his knees (on the ground) before placing his hands when he prostrated himself. And when he stood up, he raised his hands before his knees. (Using translation from Abū Dāʾūd 838)"
"1913","Sayyida Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her). A young man from among her relatives came to her who had long hair. He stood up and began to perform prayer. When he was about to go into prostration, he blew first. So Sayyida Umm Salamah said: Do not do this, because the Prophet (peace be upon him) said to one of our black boys, ""O Rabah, (during prostration) make your face dusty."
"1914","The Noble Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""When you are performing prayer, do not spread your arms the way beasts spread them. Support yourself on your palms and keep your arms away from your sides. When you do this, every part of your body will prostrate."
"1915","The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to prostrate on both of his palms."
"1916","The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: “When you go into prostration, place both your palms (on the ground) and keep both your elbows raised and set them upright.”"
"1917","when one of you prostrates himself, he should not stretch out his forearms( on the ground) like a dog and he should join both of his thighs. (Using translation from Abū Dāʾūd 901)"
"1918","The Companions of the Prophet ﷺ complained to the Prophet ﷺ about the hardship when they kept their forearms far away from their sides while prostrating. He said: Take help with the elbows (by spreading them on the ground and sticking them to the sides). (Using translation from Abū Dāʾūd 902)"
"1919","narrates: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would go into prostration, he would spread his arms so much that the whiteness of his armpits would become visible."
"1920","narrates from his father that when the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) would go into bowing (ruku'), he would keep his fingers apart, and when he would go into prostration (sajdah), he would keep his fingers together."
"1921","when a servant (of Allah) prostrates himself, the seven limbs, i.e, his face, his palms, his knees and his feet prostrate along with him. (Using translation from Abū Dāʾūd 891)"
"1922","when a servant (of Allah) prostrates himself, the seven limbs, i.e, his face, his palms, his knees and his feet prostrate along with him. (Using translation from Abū Dāʾūd 891)"
